# phiOS — sudoers drop-in for ClamAV's on-access scanner (S-xx, out-of-plan:
# clamav). Installed 0440 root:root, verified with `visudo -cf` before it is
# ever trusted — same discipline as profiles/desktop/system/etc/sudoers.d/
# 49-phi-vpn and 49-phi-firewall.
#
# `virus-event.bash` (this profile's system/etc/clamav/virus-event.bash,
# clamd.conf's VirusEvent target) runs as the unprivileged `clamav` system
# user (clamd.conf: `User clamav`) and needs to reach into every logged-in
# graphical user's own D-Bus session to raise a desktop notification —
# `sudo -u "#$USERID" ... notify-send ...` for each `/run/user/*` uid it
# finds. Without this rule that sudo call itself fails (no password, no
# tty), so a detected virus would log but never surface on screen.
#
# Scoped to exactly one binary, no wildcard arguments needed since
# virus-event.bash's own notify-send invocation is fixed. Real, corrected
# 2026-09-14: an earlier hand-edit on `zotac` put clamd.conf's `VirusEvent`
# *directive* in this file by mistake instead of an actual sudoers rule —
# a plain config line is not valid sudoers grammar, so `sudo` refused to
# parse ANY policy at all (every sudo invocation on the machine failed,
# not just clamav's) until this was replaced with the real rule below.
#
# `SETENV:` is required, not optional — confirmed live on `zotac` the same
# day: without it, sudo's default env_reset policy refuses the
# DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS/PATH assignments virus-event.bash's own
# `sudo -u "#$USERID" DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=... PATH=... notify-send`
# line sets inline ("clamav : sorry, you are not allowed to set the
# following environment variables: DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS, PATH" —
# clamd's own log, both from the daemon's real VirusEvent execution AND
# from `sudo`'s syslog line), and the notification silently never fires —
# clamd still logs the detection either way, so this failure mode is easy
# to miss without checking `journalctl -u clamav-daemon.service`.
clamav ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: SETENV: /usr/bin/notify-send
